No need to reply to this post

First of all I would like to thank the following individuals who
responded to my initial post, (which is reproduced at the bottom
of the page.)

Peter Watkinson, Eugene Chu, Kurt Ludwig, John Jasen,  Kelledin,
Maurice Hilarius, and Iwao Makino.  And anyone else I may have left out ...


The UP2000, especially one like mine with twin 750mhz/8meg CPUs
is a real power-hog.  The DCG (same as API) server box came with
a 700W Astec PSU.  Maurice says the cheapest you can get those
is $750.   The Astec is rebuildable. I found a 400W version on
Ebay, got it for $13.  It was DOA and not even a computer PSU,
but it had an 800W control board, which was an easy fit into my
PSU.  Unfortunately the 3.3V power was too far off-spec to use.


Kelledin and Kurt Ludwig both thought that a 400W PSU might run
the setup, at least for a while, maybe with a minimal number of
boards and drives.  Iwao Makino suggested the Enermax 465 or
651, and said he had sold "over a hundred clusters" with the
Enermax EG465P-VE or WG451P-VE.  Iwao also said when API first
started shipping workstations (not rackmounts) they used PC
Power and Cooling's 600 W At modified ATX PSU.  I checked, and
the spec on that PSU is **identical** to the spec in the API
user manual.

(after going over every spec I could find I did use the Enermax
EG465-AX, but more on that later)

>From the API website

http://www.microway.com/downloads/UP2000/

 I downloaded the UP2000 users and tech
reference manuals.  They both list the power requirements of the
UP2000, and they differ on specifics.  For a third opinion, on Oct 26 2002,
Maurice
Hilarius posted a msg regarding the UP2000 power supply to the
newsgroup

linux.debian.ports.alpha

and I have included his comments from that posting as well.

A switching type PSU will work with the UP2000, hook the case
power button to J44.  The PSU has to have a standard ATX
connection as well as an AT-style, 6-pin "AUX" connector, and a
free 4-pin hard-drive style connector to hook up to the MoBo.

I am summarizing the power requirements from these various
sources below:

Source                  +3.3V        +5V        +12V    +5+3.3V
API user manual         50A          70A         25A      350W
Continuous power, 500W; 600W peak power
MoBo 277W; Slot B module, 132W ea

API reference           29.8A        31A        12.5A    253.7W
Does not include drives, memory or cards
MoBo  97.05W, Ist CPU module, 165.8 W; 2nd module 142W

M Hilarius               35A          30A         15A
absolute minimum you can get by with, 450W rating

ASTEC MP6                60A          45 total     27 total
This is the PSU that came with the API 4U server; 600W rating

Some of the PSU I spec-ed out:

PSU                    +3.3V        +5V        +12V    +5+3.3V

PC Power & Cooling
425                      40             40           20     <300W
510                      30             40            34
600     $369             50             70            25    <350W
Enermax
301P                    28             30             15   170W
365P                    32             32             26   185W
465P   $105             35             35             33   200W
651     $155            36             36             36   200W

I already knew the Enermax 301P would trip out, even with all
the PCI cards pulled from the MoBo and the drives unhooked.
I took a chance on the Enermax 465P at about $120 w/ shipping.


When I hooked it up, success!  It lit the board up, the fans ran
like champions, and I waited for the LED sequence to finish out.
 It never did.
There was no video output. OK, it must be set t COM1.  I put
together a null modem cable and connected the UP2000 to a
Windoze PC with a 21-day trial version of Kermit.  Once I got
the terminal and connections sorted out, a terminal windo came
up but no signal of any kind.  I tested it by connecting a
PWS500 and booting RH 7.2 with console out put, got a signal
even if it was gibberish.

Tried resetting the SRM defaults, tried the debugging console,
nothing ever came up on the terminal.  I put in a video card and
hooked that up - never even saw the video bios come up and the
GPU heat sink never got warm.  The CPU heat sinks never got warm
either, and I never got a peep out of the speaker.  None of the
drive lights ever came on and the hard disk never spun up.

As I noted in my initial post, I had eBayed this machine and got
a "satisfaction guarantee" from PayPal.  The guarantee period
was running out, so I decided to go for it.  I told PayPal I
wasn't satisfied and they refunded my purcahse price.  All i was
out was about $100 in shipping, and another $120 for this
Enermax 450 PSU that I really don't need but can probably use.

Several people told me the 750W Alpha with 8 meg cache is the
most power hungry of any Alphas aver made, and that the later
versions take less power and run cooler.  In my snug little
80-year-old house, even an Athlon PC keeps the study nice and
toasty in the winter and forces me to open windows (real glass
pneumonia_holes, not the OS) in the summer.

The company that sold me this box had 3 of them, one of the 2
others had a "non-operative CDrom drive," but was touted as
being otherwise OK.    I think all 3 came from the same source,
all three were probably in the same under-cooled server room,
and I'll bet the other 2 are on their last legs.  FWIW Maurice
H's comment on the API 4U rackmount was something like "ugh" or
"yuck."

As a learning experience it was great.  My own thinking now is
that the UP2000 is more computer than I need, at least in
twin-chip mode.  I wouldn't rule out getting one and running it
single chip with the 667 or 833 EV6X.  But I think I'll try to
find an XP1000, maybe someplace besides eBay.

I have been getting stuff from the list but my posts have not
been going through.  I may have to post this from my work
address.

I went through the whole UP2000 PSU issue soup to nuts.  I hope
this post proves useful.

One last thing:  we are all relying on commercial websites for our
documentation - API, Compaq, HP, DEC even.  Eventually these sites will
close down, or someone will find a better use for the disk space than
hosting reams/gigabytes of documentation on obsolete product.  Maybe we
ought to start collecting and archiving all the Alpha hardware and software
info, documentation and (if possible) software we can get.

later

jn

