On Mon, 20 Oct 2003, Arkadiy Chapkis - Arc wrote:

>Interesting information. Is fedora project going to be a close
>port of RedHat? Meaning sysconfig scripts, rpm, installation,
>etc.

Speaking in an architecturally independant fashion (not Alpha 
specific)...

The Fedora Project, is the new name for what was previously known
as "Red Hat Linux", but with an expanded scope, and community
involvement.  You can read about the details of the Fedora
Project at http://fedora.redhat.com

As to being "close" to Red Hat Linux, it /is/ Red Hat Linux, only
the name has changed, and developmental policy changed.  Again,
all details are on the website.

The Alpha port that I speak of simply doesn't exist yet, and so 
there isn't much to say about it other than it is something a 
bunch of us are interested in doing sometime in the near future 
once certain things are in place, and people have time to commit 
to planning and developing.  How close this port will be to 
Fedora Core is hard to say.  The ultimate goal is for it to _be_ 
an official Fedora Core port named "Fedora Core / Alpha".  I 
definitely would like the port to track Fedora 100%, meaning the 
same packages, etc.


>Also, puting my 2 cents into it - I am not a
>developer/coder/programmer, however I do own a couple alphas,
>which I can give an access to developers/coders/programmers for
>developing/compiling/testing the ports. Also, I guess I can help
>with documentation.

If you're interested, that'd be great when the time comes.  I'm 
sure we'll need and want more volunteers, but we really need to 
get the basics ready first, and it's not even remotely started 
yet.  I've only mentioned it here, just to let people know that 
something is brewing in the hearts and minds of some of us.  ;o)

Hope this helps a bit..   Let me know if you've got any other 
questions that I might be able to answer/explain, and also, keep 
your fingers crossed.  ;o)

Take care,
TTYL


-- 
