To the list:

The DEC 3000 series machines are old and slow, but if you've had experience
with them you appreciate their durability and stability.  I learned my GIS
chops on a 3000/600, which was the first workstation you could do a "clean"
or "build" operation on a complex polygon cover and not have time for a cup
of coffee.

I set one up last year with Tru64 and the CDE xserver and it ran pretty
respectably.  My guess is that KDE or Gnome would bog those machines down
badly ... just a suggestion, if you do the port include somehing like CDE
for the Xserver.

jn

