>Subject: Re: [ADMIN] futher list cleanups
>
>On Thu, Oct 23, 2003 at 05:36:47PM -0400, Donsbach, Jeff wrote:
>> Don't forget there is also a debian alpha list, a suse alpha list and a gentoo alpha list. Any others I'm forgetting?
>
>Yes I know, and it's no good. We should have one "central" list,
>and it should be open for non-subscribers, like linux-kernel list.

Not everyone in the world wants to be on wide open mailing lists 
that get 10000 spam per day.  I despise wide open lists.  Even 
_with_ spam assassin, I get over 30 spam in my INBOX per day, and 
I don't have time to tweak spam assassin on a daily basis for 20 
minutes to handle the latest tricky spam annoyances.

People join a Red Hat Alpha list to discuss issues related to Red 
Hat Linux on Alpha platforms, of course one doesn't need to be 
using Red Hat Linux or any Red Hat OS to be welcome here.  That 
goes without saying.  ;o)

If people want to read Debian/SuSE/whatever specific stuff,
they'd be best off subscribing to the Alpha mailing lists of
those particular distributions.  If people want to read generic 
all encompassing mail about Alpha, perhaps linux-alpha is for 
them.  If people want spam-enabled list that allows posting from 
every spam machine out there and no list out there does that, 
they're free to set up GNU mailman and create such a list of 
course.  ;o)

This list is officially (by list charter) for Red Hat Linux on 
Alpha processors though, even though there isn't any strictness 
about that, and off topic alpha posts are generally always 
welcome.  I'd hate to see the list all of a sudden become a wide 
open spam trap, as no other Red Hat lists are wide open - for 
good reason.

Also, while Red Hat hasn't produced an Alpha distribution for 
quite a while now (7.2), a number of us here have plans to work 
on Alpha again in the near future, and produce an Alpha 
distribution again, under the Fedora Project: http://fedora.redhat.com

This list is likely to be the official discussion list for that 
once it gets off the ground.  Alternatively we could create a new 
list 'fedora-alpha-list' I suppose, but then people don't want 
1000 alpha related mailing lists do they?  ;o)

Take care,
TTYL

-- 
