>Sure, posting to such lists does increase amount of spam directed
>to e-mail address of a sender, but same is true for closed lists
>which have public web archives.

Not if the public archives strip or obfuscate email addresses.

>OTOH, one of serious disadvantages of closed lists is that you cannot
>CC: non-subscriber - it's just rude.

Not an important enough problem to warrant making a list open in 
my eyes.


>> People join a Red Hat Alpha list to discuss issues related to Red 
>> Hat Linux on Alpha platforms, of course one doesn't need to be 
>> using Red Hat Linux or any Red Hat OS to be welcome here.  That 
>> goes without saying.  ;o)
>
>Surely, distribution-specific lists must exist, but a lot of
>problems are really generic since all distributions use mostly
>the same source base. And discussing such problems only on private
>lists often leads to duplicated work.

But you're unlikely to be able to join all 15 or whatever Alpha 
lists out there and convince 100% or even 60% of the 
subscribership that your way is the best way.  Different people 
have different tastes.  By all means feel free to persue or 
create a one-list-to-rule-them-all, but you're unlikely to get 
the complete subscriberships of all other lists to join the one 
true list and unsubscribe from any others.  The reason individual 
lists exist is usually subsetting or topic refinement.  There 
will always be overlap, that's unavoidable as human nature.


>>  Alternatively we could create a new 
>> list 'fedora-alpha-list' I suppose, but then people don't want 
>> 1000 alpha related mailing lists do they?  ;o)
>
>IMHO, even those 7-8 mentioned here are too much. ;-)

You're free to pick one list that you consider the official 
alpha-everything list though and join just that one list if you 
like.  I'm not particularly interested in that myself, so I 
probably wont join, at least not currently.  Others probably will 
join, perhaps some will just move to that list, others will stay 
here.  If you're not here, you wont catch what is said here.  ;o)

I'm on over 200 mailing lists right now, I don't want to join new 
lists if I can avoid it, and I prefer lists that narrow in on the 
specific topic I'm interested in.  I'm not really interested in 
generic Alpha problems on various distributions/OS's out there, 
just Alpha/Red Hat discussion content.  ;o)

Most likely there are 8-10 or more opinions from people on this 
list that differ from both mine and yours.  That's why there are 
so many lists to begin with... different strokes for different 
folks.  ;o)


-- 
