On Tue, Oct 28, 2003 at 12:09:34AM -0500, Arkadiy Chapkis - Arc wrote:
>
> OK, I finally got sound to work. Unfortunately I had to go back to
> OSS modules, as neither one of ALSA worked for me. Unfortunately,
> because it seems that OSS will be going away and the best I could
> get with ALSA snd_sb8 is some hissing sound while playing with
> aplay. Not to mention that /dev/snd/pcmC0D0* weren't available (I
> made the with MAKDEV.snd, but couldn't redirect a sound file to
> those). I tried every dma setting and had to use alsamixer to upped
> the channels every time I loaded modules.

OK, I just went back to duplicate what I'd done before to get it working
on my MIATA, and that was (under 2.6.0-test4):

1. modprobe -v snd-sb8 port=0x220 irq=5 dma8=1

This should succeed with no error messages, and result in quite a few
modules getting loaded.

2. modprobe -v snd-pcm-oss

This loads up the two OSS-compatibility modules needed by the ALSA stuff
in order to handle the apps that were built only for the OSS API.

3. aumix

Adjusted all sound levels (VOL/SYNTH/PCM), as they were initially 0.

4. playwave /usr/share/sounds/KDE_Startup_new.wav

This should be audible.

5. XMMS

I used this to play several MP3 files I had lying around.

Note that the rear-panel speaker output jack was a good bit loader than
the front-panel headphone jack, for some reason.

All this was under a pretty-much stock RH 7.2 for Alpha install with
updates through May 2003, and used a 2.6.0-test4 kernel with sound
modules.

Good luck.

