On Fri, Oct 31, 2003 at 11:57:18AM +0100, Stefan Reinauer wrote:
> * Luke Brennan <lukeb@microsoft.com> [031031 11:47]:
> > I'm using boot sda1:vmlinux-2.4.9-32.5 root=/dev/sda9  which is the same
> > as I used for 7.1 but with the new kernel. 

You need initrd here to load drivers for your SCSI disk or that disk
will be not visible to your kernel.  Older installations used to have a
few "popular" SCSI drivers built-in in kernels and now they are all
modules.

> what milo version are you using? does the kernel complain? then it's
> probably an initrd issue. Milo should be able to handle EXT3 as an EXT2
> filesystem, ignoring the journal

Somehow I doubt that milos, with the newest versions based on 2.2
kernels, will read a file system formatted with a block size 4096 and/or
sparse superblocks.  In this particular case a "conversion" really
consisted from adding a journal so these are not problems.  In any case
the trouble mentioned is past this stage.  Still in general it is better
to have a small boot partition, different than /, formatted in such way
that milo surely will not have troubles accessing it.  That means
ext2 and '-b 1024 -O none'.  For "sufficiently small" file systems
these options should be default.

It is also wise, especially with milos, to use HPWrapper CD during
installations/upgrades.  In such case you would end up not only with a
kernel newer than a looong time obsolete 2.4.9-32.5 but also milo.conf
would be written for you by an installer.  Then you could use at milo
prompt 'b sda1:' and save yourself a lot of typing and guessing.

Binary RPM files of updates for your "new" installation currently will
roughly fill a CD.

   Michal



